WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.701 --> 00:00:02.968
-Our first guest tonight is an
Emmy-winning actor and director

00:00:02.968 --> 00:00:04.133
you know from his work
on shows

00:00:04.133 --> 00:00:05.667
such as "Ozark,"
"Arrested Development,"

00:00:05.667 --> 00:00:07.367
as well as
his podcast "SmartLess."

00:00:07.367 --> 00:00:10.067
He stars in "Air," which will
be in theaters April 5th.

00:00:10.067 --> 00:00:11.100
Let's take a look.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:14.033
-51% of a shoe
has to be white.

00:00:14.033 --> 00:00:17.434
NBA is extremely strict
about it. They'll fine him.

00:00:17.434 --> 00:00:20.334
-They'll fine him?
-$5,000 a game.

00:00:22.701 --> 00:00:24.467
We're [bleep].

00:00:29.234 --> 00:00:31.300
-What about more red?

00:00:33.567 --> 00:00:36.868
A lot more red.

00:00:36.868 --> 00:00:40.067
And what about if we
just pay the fines?

00:00:40.067 --> 00:00:41.734
Make a commercial out of it?

00:00:41.734 --> 00:00:43.534
You know,
it'll give us headlines.

00:00:43.534 --> 00:00:46.901
They're going to fine Michael
Jordan for being too colorful.

00:00:46.901 --> 00:00:49.534
-Please welcome to the show
Jason Bateman, everybody.

00:00:49.534 --> 00:00:50.968
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:50.968 --> 00:00:59.801
♪♪

00:00:59.801 --> 00:01:08.701
♪♪

00:01:08.701 --> 00:01:10.968
Welcome to the show, my friend.

00:01:10.968 --> 00:01:12.200
-Seth.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:13.334
-It's very nice
to have you here.

00:01:13.334 --> 00:01:15.033
-It's been a little too long.

00:01:15.033 --> 00:01:17.334
-It's been a little bit
too long since I last saw you.

00:01:17.334 --> 00:01:19.901
You know, I think maybe
the last time I saw you --

00:01:19.901 --> 00:01:21.400
I've seen you since --
but the last time I saw you

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:25.534
in this physical building was
when you hosted "SNL" in 2005.

00:01:25.534 --> 00:01:27.400
-Yes.
-You've come back since,

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:28.634
but after I left.

00:01:28.634 --> 00:01:29.834
-Yes.
-Yes.

00:01:29.834 --> 00:01:31.300
It was not as much fun
without me.

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:33.234
-I said "I'm not coming back
till he goes."

00:01:33.234 --> 00:01:34.601
-Okay.
-You know?

00:01:34.601 --> 00:01:36.067
They accommodated.
-So it was the opposite

00:01:36.067 --> 00:01:37.267
of what I thought it was?

00:01:37.267 --> 00:01:38.567
I thought it was hard
for you to come back.

00:01:38.567 --> 00:01:41.334
It was super easy.
-The truth is hurtful.

00:01:41.334 --> 00:01:43.701
Now, what's going on here at
12:30?

00:01:43.701 --> 00:01:45.868
There was
an F-bomb in the clip.

00:01:45.868 --> 00:01:47.868
-Yeah.
-I heard you drop an S-bomb

00:01:47.868 --> 00:01:49.734
in the "Closer Look."
-Yeah.

00:01:49.734 --> 00:01:52.434
-Do those go unbleeped?

00:01:52.434 --> 00:01:53.734
-It turns out the censor

00:01:53.734 --> 00:01:55.434
always falls asleep
right before it starts.

00:01:55.434 --> 00:01:56.801
[ Laughter ]

00:01:56.801 --> 00:01:58.734
-So you guys can just run amok?

00:01:58.734 --> 00:02:01.234
-We run [bleep].
I'll just say it.

00:02:01.234 --> 00:02:03.000
There you go.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:04.033
I don't care.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:04.033 --> 00:02:05.133
-They love it.
-What are they gonna do?

00:02:05.133 --> 00:02:07.767
-They love it.
-They love it. Look.

00:02:07.767 --> 00:02:09.567
It does get beeped. Bleeped.

00:02:09.567 --> 00:02:10.701
I've been told it's bleep.
-I've got my 11-year-old

00:02:10.701 --> 00:02:12.434
daughter here, so let's mind it.

00:02:12.434 --> 00:02:15.968
-Yeah. I'm sorry.
-No, she --

00:02:15.968 --> 00:02:17.000
-But she must understand it,
right?

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:18.701
-She can drop a pretty hot
F-bomb.

00:02:18.701 --> 00:02:19.801
[ Laughter ]

00:02:19.801 --> 00:02:21.100
-Well, she has
an older sister, right?

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:23.234
So if you've got
a 16-year-old sister

00:02:23.234 --> 00:02:25.667
and you're 11,
you've heard the F-bombs.

00:02:25.667 --> 00:02:27.000
-Yeah.
And there was like, what --

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:30.467
So your kids are...
-Seven, five and one.

00:02:30.467 --> 00:02:32.133
-Are you cursing around them?

00:02:32.133 --> 00:02:34.400
-No, but they're learning it.

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:35.734
-You have never cursed
around them?

00:02:35.734 --> 00:02:37.000
-I mean, I think I've cursed
around them,

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:38.868
but they live near a park
in New York City.

00:02:38.868 --> 00:02:40.234
It's not like...
[ Laughter ]

00:02:40.234 --> 00:02:42.000
-What, are they moving
dime bags out there?

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:43.634
[ Laughter ]

00:02:43.634 --> 00:02:44.968
How are they hearing...

00:02:44.968 --> 00:02:46.534
-They have internships

00:02:46.534 --> 00:02:48.100
with some of the,
like, lower-end dealers.

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:49.434
-I got it.
So they're just packaging.

00:02:49.434 --> 00:02:50.901
-'Cause I told them, "Don't go
out there

00:02:50.901 --> 00:02:52.834
and just think you can, like,
take over a corner."

00:02:52.834 --> 00:02:53.868
-Sure.
-Like, you know.

00:02:53.868 --> 00:02:55.200
-They're probably packaging

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:56.834
and they're giving to the guy.

00:02:56.834 --> 00:02:59.467
-It helps them learn
just more responsibility.

00:02:59.467 --> 00:03:01.801
Because I'll tell you this.
You can lie to me,

00:03:01.801 --> 00:03:03.901
but you cannot lie
to the guys who run that corner.

00:03:03.901 --> 00:03:05.300
[ Laughter ]

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:07.467
-Now, when you do
your disciplining,

00:03:07.467 --> 00:03:10.400
do you ever slip
and curse and say --

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:11.901
Is there even a "God damn it"?

00:03:11.901 --> 00:03:14.067
-No, I don't think there's
a God d--

00:03:14.067 --> 00:03:16.734
I'm very good. The worst thing.
Did this happen to you?

00:03:16.734 --> 00:03:18.934
The worst thing is
when I get mad at my kids,

00:03:18.934 --> 00:03:21.467
they laugh at me.
There's something.

00:03:21.467 --> 00:03:23.868
I think I get mad
like a cartoon.

00:03:23.868 --> 00:03:25.634
-Yeah.
-Oh, do you got her over here?

00:03:25.634 --> 00:03:27.334
-Oh, yeah. She's watching
the way it's made.

00:03:27.334 --> 00:03:28.634
-Oh, God.
So she actually came into

00:03:28.634 --> 00:03:30.467
the studio to hear my F-bombs.
I'm so sorry.

00:03:30.467 --> 00:03:32.501
-Exactly.

00:03:32.501 --> 00:03:34.267
-I made it very clear.

00:03:34.267 --> 00:03:35.567
When you bring children
under 13,

00:03:35.567 --> 00:03:37.634
I like them to wear
noise-canceling headphones...

00:03:37.634 --> 00:03:39.167
[ Laughter ]

00:03:39.167 --> 00:03:40.601
...for the entirety
of the interview.

00:03:40.601 --> 00:03:42.334
-That would be smart.

00:03:42.334 --> 00:03:45.400
-This is a film
about Nike and Michael Jordan.

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:48.734
Very beautiful '80s flourishes
throughout this whole film.

00:03:48.734 --> 00:03:51.133
-To the extent there
is beauty in the '80s, yes.

00:03:51.133 --> 00:03:52.400
-Yes.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:54.033
This was a decade you enjoyed.

00:03:54.033 --> 00:03:58.234
-I did well in the '80s.
But it is tacky.

00:03:58.234 --> 00:03:59.901
-Yeah.
-The '80s is tacky.

00:03:59.901 --> 00:04:02.300
There was a kitsch factor
to the '80s, right?

00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:05.667
The "Miami Vice" turquoise
and the Day-Glo.

00:04:05.667 --> 00:04:08.467
-Do you think we knew it was
kitsch at the time, though?

00:04:08.467 --> 00:04:10.467
-Well, clearly,
I think it's not kitsch

00:04:10.467 --> 00:04:13.234
because this is still here.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:13.234 --> 00:04:14.834
-You've muted your colors,

00:04:14.834 --> 00:04:16.834
but otherwise this
is "Miami Vice."

00:04:16.834 --> 00:04:18.767
-Yeah, it's like I'm waiting
for the reshoots,

00:04:18.767 --> 00:04:20.868
but I don't think
I get the concept of the film

00:04:20.868 --> 00:04:22.701
that's already in the theaters.

00:04:22.701 --> 00:04:24.634
So I can cut it now.
-They might want to go back.

00:04:24.634 --> 00:04:26.133
They might want to go
back and pick anything up.

00:04:26.133 --> 00:04:27.701
How were your '90s?

00:04:27.701 --> 00:04:31.334
-The '90s were quiet.
-Okay.

00:04:31.334 --> 00:04:33.667
-Except for my nightlife.

00:04:33.667 --> 00:04:34.667
-Oh, interesting.

00:04:34.667 --> 00:04:36.834
-Yeah, that doesn't usually
dovetail

00:04:36.834 --> 00:04:38.434
with a rise in the career
at the same time.

00:04:38.434 --> 00:04:41.100
-Yeah. No, the career usually
plummets if you're out at night.

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:44.300
-Yeah.
-And the golf handicap goes up.

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:45.601
-Well, there you go.
-You know?

00:04:45.601 --> 00:04:47.400
-The 2000s, you hit the 2000s
hard.

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:49.033
-2000s did alright.
-You were back.

00:04:49.033 --> 00:04:50.734
-Because I got
"Arrested Development"

00:04:50.734 --> 00:04:52.734
and that sort of hit
the reset for me.

00:04:52.734 --> 00:04:53.734
-Well, there you go.

00:04:53.734 --> 00:04:55.767
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:55.767 --> 00:04:58.934
A nice way. A very nice --
-I got very lucky.

00:04:58.934 --> 00:05:01.100
-A nice reset, that show.

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:04.000
-I mean, I would have done
a show half as good, you know?

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:05.567
Like, I was just looking
for a job.

00:05:05.567 --> 00:05:07.801
-I got to be honest.
And I mean this.

00:05:07.801 --> 00:05:09.267
There weren't even
a lot of shows

00:05:09.267 --> 00:05:10.634
half as good
as "Arrested Development."

00:05:10.634 --> 00:05:12.000
That's how good
"Arrested Development" was.

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:14.968
-Mitch Hurwitz
is a real genius, that fella.

00:05:14.968 --> 00:05:16.334
Then there's that
Will Arnett on there.

00:05:16.334 --> 00:05:18.968
But we still succeeded
despite him.

00:05:18.968 --> 00:05:22.734
-It was lovely.
-Yeah, bless his heart.

00:05:22.734 --> 00:05:24.400
-It's lovely,
though that you guys,

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:27.000
you and Will obviously
are still working together.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:28.133
Sean Hayes as well.

00:05:28.133 --> 00:05:29.901
-Yeah, we got this podcast
going.

00:05:29.901 --> 00:05:31.767
-An incredible podcast.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:31.767 --> 00:05:33.567
Incredible triumph.

00:05:33.567 --> 00:05:35.667
And one of the most
lovely things

00:05:35.667 --> 00:05:37.000
is you're very supportive
of each other,

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:40.200
and Sean is going to be
on Broadway.

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:42.167
He's got a show
that's coming here.

00:05:42.167 --> 00:05:44.434
-Yes, It's called
"Good Night, Oscar."

00:05:44.434 --> 00:05:45.467
-I saw it in Chicago.

00:05:45.467 --> 00:05:46.801
-Yeah.
-It's incredible.

00:05:46.801 --> 00:05:48.634
-It's about a guy
that wins an Oscar

00:05:48.634 --> 00:05:50.968
and sleeps with the Oscar
like a stuffed animal?

00:05:50.968 --> 00:05:52.734
-I think you...
[ Laughter ]

00:05:52.734 --> 00:05:55.100
No, that was the screenplay
I sent you.

00:05:55.100 --> 00:05:56.367
-Yes.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:56.367 --> 00:05:58.601
Sorry.
-That you did not
give me feedback on yet.

00:05:58.601 --> 00:06:03.567
-I'd check your e-mails.
-Okay.

00:06:03.567 --> 00:06:04.968
-Are you going to go check out?
-There's going to

00:06:04.968 --> 00:06:06.868
be a pilgrimage out here
for opening night,

00:06:06.868 --> 00:06:10.267
which is April 24th, 25th,
something like that, guys.

00:06:10.267 --> 00:06:12.667
-Okay, gotcha.
-Check it out, please.

00:06:12.667 --> 00:06:14.367
-It's very exciting.
It's a wonderful show.

00:06:14.367 --> 00:06:17.367
And he's incredible in it.
-You did see it?

00:06:17.367 --> 00:06:19.901
-I did see it. I saw it
in Chicago in previews. Yeah.

00:06:19.901 --> 00:06:21.067
-Yeah.
Apparently it was good enough

00:06:21.067 --> 00:06:22.501
to make it out here.
-Yeah.

00:06:22.501 --> 00:06:24.467
-I mean, they were in Chicago
and everybody was like,

00:06:24.467 --> 00:06:25.901
"This is good. We should
bring this to New York."

00:06:25.901 --> 00:06:27.834
-So I don't have to worry
about coming up

00:06:27.834 --> 00:06:29.834
with something
falsely complimentary backstage?

00:06:29.834 --> 00:06:31.634
-Interesting.
That's your concern?

00:06:31.634 --> 00:06:32.801
-Well, here's the thing.

00:06:32.801 --> 00:06:34.968
From what I understand,

00:06:34.968 --> 00:06:39.200
on Broadway,
if you are semi-recognizable

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:41.400
or basically have a SAG card...

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:43.234
-If you maybe had a moment
in the '80s.

00:06:43.234 --> 00:06:45.667
-Right.
And you're in the audience

00:06:45.667 --> 00:06:46.968
and you watch the show,

00:06:46.968 --> 00:06:50.567
if you don't go backstage
and say hi or something,

00:06:50.567 --> 00:06:54.167
that is tantamount
to giving it a pan, like that

00:06:54.167 --> 00:06:56.767
you hated it because apparently
the people in the show,

00:06:56.767 --> 00:06:59.234
they know if you're semi-famous,
if you're in the audience,

00:06:59.234 --> 00:07:00.501
and you're supposed to go back.

00:07:00.501 --> 00:07:01.934
I think that
that's really presumptuous.

00:07:01.934 --> 00:07:04.334
Like, I don't know the people
that are in the show.

00:07:04.334 --> 00:07:07.634
It's like, "Oh, I'm famous,
you're famous. We should talk."

00:07:07.634 --> 00:07:10.367
That's cringey, right?
That's very cringey.

00:07:10.367 --> 00:07:12.801
But apparently, no, that's what
you're supposed to do.

00:07:12.801 --> 00:07:15.601
And Sean's case,
I will do it anyway.

00:07:15.601 --> 00:07:18.434
-And are you concerned that if
you don't like the show,

00:07:18.434 --> 00:07:20.300
that Sean will be able
to clock your insincerity?

00:07:20.300 --> 00:07:22.601
-Yeah, I've got skills, but I'm
not that good of an actor.

00:07:22.601 --> 00:07:23.834
[ Laughter ]

00:07:23.834 --> 00:07:25.000
-Do you want to, like, practice
on me?

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:26.801
-Want to workshop something?

00:07:26.801 --> 00:07:29.534
-Yeah. Like, so I'll be Sean.
-Right.

00:07:29.534 --> 00:07:31.033
-And it's after the show
and I want --

00:07:31.033 --> 00:07:33.267
So your backstory here
is you really didn't like it.

00:07:33.267 --> 00:07:34.734
-Did not like the show?
-Yeah, and you thought

00:07:34.734 --> 00:07:36.267
the biggest problem
was Sean's performance.

00:07:36.267 --> 00:07:37.501
-Right, right. Here we go.

00:07:37.501 --> 00:07:38.868
-Alright, I'll open
the dressing room.

00:07:38.868 --> 00:07:40.267
Okay?
-Okay.

00:07:40.267 --> 00:07:42.467
Sean!

00:07:42.467 --> 00:07:44.000
Oh, my God.

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:46.667
[ Laughter ]

00:07:46.667 --> 00:07:49.200
Ah. You've already changed.

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:51.033
[ Laughter ]

00:07:51.033 --> 00:07:52.100
Right?
-No.

00:07:52.100 --> 00:07:53.367
-We talk about everything
but the show.

00:07:53.367 --> 00:07:54.501
-That's really good, though.

00:07:54.501 --> 00:07:56.501
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:56.501 --> 00:07:57.934
"You've already changed."

00:07:57.934 --> 00:08:00.100
-You've already changed.
-"You've already changed."

00:08:00.100 --> 00:08:02.501
-Oh, you must be exhausted.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:02.501 --> 00:08:06.200
-I think the real giveaway is
that you bought yourself time

00:08:06.200 --> 00:08:08.200
by saying his name.
-Right.

00:08:08.200 --> 00:08:09.801
-Which he clearly knows
you know.

00:08:09.801 --> 00:08:11.167
-And I elongated it.
-Yes.

00:08:11.167 --> 00:08:12.634
-Seeean!
-Seeean!

00:08:12.634 --> 00:08:15.701
-As you're trying
to think up something to say.

00:08:15.701 --> 00:08:16.734
-To go back to the movie
for a second,

00:08:16.734 --> 00:08:17.834
this is, you know,
Michael Jordan.

00:08:17.834 --> 00:08:19.167
This is the Bulls.
You're an L.A. guy.

00:08:19.167 --> 00:08:21.634
Were you a Laker fan
during the Jordan years?

00:08:21.634 --> 00:08:22.868
-There was a...

00:08:22.868 --> 00:08:26.434
I was pretty cheesy in the '90s.

00:08:26.434 --> 00:08:28.734
More cheesy than I am now,
let's say.

00:08:28.734 --> 00:08:31.801
And I went ahead and I bought
two seats at the Forum,

00:08:31.801 --> 00:08:34.200
which is a cheesy thing to do.
I mean, not cheesy.

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:36.334
It's just like
it's "I'm big time."

00:08:36.334 --> 00:08:40.033
-It's a little show-off.
-Yeah, I wouldn't do that today.

00:08:40.033 --> 00:08:41.334
I do like going
to sporting events,

00:08:41.334 --> 00:08:44.434
but these seats
had like your plaque,

00:08:44.434 --> 00:08:46.601
like your name underneath it.
They were called Senate seats.

00:08:46.601 --> 00:08:49.167
So you own those seats
for everything at the Forum.

00:08:49.167 --> 00:08:51.968
This is the place where
they played before the Crypto.

00:08:51.968 --> 00:08:54.634
And so I was there for every
Laker game, every Kings game,

00:08:54.634 --> 00:08:56.200
every boxing match,
every concert.

00:08:56.200 --> 00:08:59.200
I mean, it was -- I had a little
credit card for the Forum Club,

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:02.400
the bar inside,
so that I could really cut down

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:05.033
on the time it takes
to get inebriated.

00:09:05.033 --> 00:09:08.267
You know? It was a fun decade,

00:09:08.267 --> 00:09:10.234
but not very prosperous.

00:09:10.234 --> 00:09:13.667
-And when it was over, did
they give you the chair?

00:09:13.667 --> 00:09:15.234
Did they give you
your two seats?

00:09:15.234 --> 00:09:16.901
-No, they just sold it
to the next cheesy guy.

00:09:16.901 --> 00:09:18.367
[ Laughter ]

00:09:18.367 --> 00:09:20.501
-They were prying off the plaque
with a screwdriver.

00:09:20.501 --> 00:09:22.167
-You run out
of money after living

00:09:22.167 --> 00:09:23.968
like that for a while.
They take the chair back.

00:09:23.968 --> 00:09:24.968
-When they say, "Mr. Bateman,

00:09:24.968 --> 00:09:26.267
your Forum card has been
denied."

00:09:26.267 --> 00:09:27.667
-Yeah, exactly.

00:09:27.667 --> 00:09:29.567
"We'll take that back, please."

00:09:29.567 --> 00:09:30.667
But yeah, I got to see a lot.

00:09:30.667 --> 00:09:32.467
I got to see Jordan play
there quite a bit.

00:09:32.467 --> 00:09:34.701
-Were you one of those L.A.
actors at the time

00:09:34.701 --> 00:09:38.200
who was playing
in the pickup basketball games?

00:09:38.200 --> 00:09:40.734
-There was a little bit
of a group of those.

00:09:40.734 --> 00:09:43.901
Yeah. I was working at
Warner Bros.

00:09:43.901 --> 00:09:47.133
George Clooney had a game there
right behind the "ER" stage.

00:09:47.133 --> 00:09:48.801
-There you go.
I've always heard about that.

00:09:48.801 --> 00:09:50.567
That must have been a pretty
cool game to be a part of.

00:09:50.567 --> 00:09:51.701
-Well, I was
not a part of that.

00:09:51.701 --> 00:09:55.300
-Okay.
[ Laughter ]

00:09:55.300 --> 00:09:57.133
-You were there.
You were physically nearby.

00:09:57.133 --> 00:09:59.334
-When they got thirsty,
they'd go,

00:09:59.334 --> 00:10:00.534
"And we'll take the bottle."

00:10:00.534 --> 00:10:01.868
And so I'd give the water.

00:10:01.868 --> 00:10:04.567
No, I would do
little drive-bys, you know,

00:10:04.567 --> 00:10:06.601
to see if they needed
that extra player.

00:10:06.601 --> 00:10:09.067
-Is that in sort of
Hollywood studio lot golf cart?

00:10:09.067 --> 00:10:10.067
-Exactly, yeah.

00:10:10.067 --> 00:10:11.567
With no horn.
So you kind of go,

00:10:11.567 --> 00:10:15.200
"Hey, beep, beep, guys.
Anybody...?

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:17.868
Want to...?"
[ Laughter ]

00:10:17.868 --> 00:10:20.634
No, Noah Wyle and and George

00:10:20.634 --> 00:10:22.100
were just fine playing
one-on-one.

00:10:22.100 --> 00:10:23.901
-Yeah.
I mean, you got to really be

00:10:23.901 --> 00:10:25.400
willing to suffer
through awkwardness

00:10:25.400 --> 00:10:27.667
if you let a man slowly
go by in a golf cart.

00:10:27.667 --> 00:10:28.868
[ Laughter ]

00:10:28.868 --> 00:10:30.267
-Just doing tight circles.

00:10:30.267 --> 00:10:32.634
Just doing figure eights out
in front of it.

00:10:32.634 --> 00:10:34.868
-I want to close
because you were in a sketch

00:10:34.868 --> 00:10:36.434
I think about
all the time in 2005.

00:10:36.434 --> 00:10:37.634
I'm going to bring it back
to the beginning.

00:10:37.634 --> 00:10:39.133
When we met at "SNL" --
We didn't meet,

00:10:39.133 --> 00:10:40.734
but the last time we saw
each other in this building.

00:10:40.734 --> 00:10:42.133
And it was --
Do you remember T.T. and Mario?

00:10:42.133 --> 00:10:43.133
Do you remember that sketch?

00:10:43.133 --> 00:10:45.234
-T.T. and Mario. I do not.

00:10:45.234 --> 00:10:48.067
-You and Amy Poehler --
-Was I T.T. or Mario?

00:10:48.067 --> 00:10:49.234
-You were neither.
-Okay.

00:10:49.234 --> 00:10:50.601
[ Laughter ]

00:10:50.601 --> 00:10:52.501
-You guys were doing
an album ad

00:10:52.501 --> 00:10:55.267
for a very famous soul group
called T.T. and Mario,

00:10:55.267 --> 00:10:57.934
and it was like one of those
like sort of '70 record album

00:10:57.934 --> 00:10:59.434
where you're like,
"Do you remember this hit song?"

00:10:59.434 --> 00:11:02.033
And so you introduced a song,
and this is a song

00:11:02.033 --> 00:11:04.701
that was so beautiful
and moving to me --

00:11:04.701 --> 00:11:05.968
and this is a true story --

00:11:05.968 --> 00:11:07.934
my wife and I put it
on our wedding playlist.

00:11:07.934 --> 00:11:09.133
-Come on.
-Yeah.

00:11:09.133 --> 00:11:10.501
And I think for everybody
who doesn't remember,

00:11:10.501 --> 00:11:12.400
this is a really gorgeous song.
Can we just watch a clip?

00:11:12.400 --> 00:11:13.801
-Let's do it.

00:11:13.801 --> 00:11:15.067
-I know you're going to know
this one.

00:11:15.067 --> 00:11:19.701
Who didn't groove on this bad
mama jama in 1977?

00:11:19.701 --> 00:11:21.467
"Roller Skate Love."

00:11:21.467 --> 00:11:24.534
♪♪

00:11:24.534 --> 00:11:26.567
-♪ You are so beautiful ♪

00:11:26.567 --> 00:11:29.701
-♪ And you are my knight
in shining armor ♪

00:11:29.701 --> 00:11:31.434
-♪ Let me put it in
a little bit ♪

00:11:31.434 --> 00:11:32.767
-♪ Just the tip ♪

00:11:32.767 --> 00:11:35.100
[ Laughter ]

00:11:35.100 --> 00:11:36.601
-Really beautiful.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:11:36.601 --> 00:11:40.801
Really beautiful.
It was our first dance.

00:11:40.801 --> 00:11:44.033
What a joy to have you back.
It is so lovely to see you.

00:11:44.033 --> 00:11:45.334
Thanks for making
the time for us, my friend.

00:11:45.334 --> 00:11:48.434
-I'm so glad to be here.
Thank you, buddy.

00:11:48.434 --> 00:11:50.801
-That's Jason Bateman,
everybody. "Air" premieres

00:11:50.801 --> 00:11:52.367
globally tomorrow.
We'll be right back

00:11:52.367 --> 00:11:55.467
with Congresswoman
Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez.

